Z||000006||pHoOl||10-17-2001||12:54 PM||||Is this a problem on 88s too?  Cause i noticed something with my fronts that sounded like bump steer (going to read the explanation).  Just trying to figure out if it's the is mysterious bump steer, or ball joints wearing out.<P>------------------<BR>Rice mobiles are like tampons... Every p@#%y has one.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/phoolsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfixit58||10-17-2001||01:47 PM||||The simple explanation is when the rear wheels hit a bump the rear suspension geometer changes such the that the rear of the car tries to steer itself.<P>More technically, as the suspension travels through it's travel, the "toe" changes. Think of a typical front end alignment. There are basically three adjustments: caster, camber, and toe. Toe is the measurement of the distance between the front of the front tires and the back of the front tires. Ideally, you want them parallel (or, at least, very close) to reduce tire wear and to keep the car tracking straight down the road.<P>On 84-87 Fieros, the rear suspension is, for all practical purposes, the front of a Chevy Citation with the steering hubs locked in place. Unfortunately, the arm that locks the hub in place, is not the same length (or rather, doesn't travel through the same arc)as the "A" arm. Therefore, as the suspension runs through it's travel, the arm will pull or push the hub in or out thereby causing the that wheel to try and steer the rear of the car.<P>As I understand it, the 88 re-design took car of most of this problem.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroRacer||10-17-2001||01:57 PM||||And here I thought it was a prototype for a 4 wheel steering system for our cars? <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||watts||10-18-2001||02:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>As I understand it, the 88 re-design took car of most of this problem.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>On the 84-87's, the toe link is attached to the cradle.  When the wheel/suspension/etc moves through it's arc, the wheel tries to turn since the toe link is attached to a non moving object (the cradle).<P>In 88, the toe link is gone.  And so is the problem.<P>I've got my new 88 cradle sitting on the floor of my shop all in pieces right now (cleaning, fixing, etc) getting ready to go into my 87.  I'll make sure to take lots of photo's of it if anyone wants/needs any.<BR>||||reg||1
